  "name": "Question 11",
  "problem": "What is the remainder when  $27{x}^{3}+8$ is divided by $9{x}^{2}-6x+4?$",
  "worked_solution": "Notice that the dividend is missing the ${x}^{2}$ and $x$ terms. So, we first rewrite it by including the missing terms with zero coefficients, as follows:\n\n$$\n27{x}^{3}+8=27{x}^{3}+0{x}^{2}+0x+8\n$$\n\nNow, using the method of polynomial division, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,3x+2 \\\\  & 9{x}^{2}-6x+4\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{\\,\\,27{x}^{3}+0{x}^{2}+0x+8\\,\\,} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{27{x}^{3}-18{x}^{2}+12x}_{\u2015} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,18{x}^{2}-12x+8 \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{18{x}^{2}-12x+8}_{\u2015} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n- the quotient is $3x+2,$ and\n\n- the remainder is $0.$",
